1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,060
So we've now gotten through all the lectures on machine code and we're starting to talk about, okay, now that you know this stuff, what can you do with it?

2
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:14,220
And this lecture is along the lines of that.

3
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:20,800
This is sort of what you are now empowered to do now that you can look at and understand machine code.

4
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:29,980
This material is a little, this actually, there's a whole chapter of the book, Chapter 5 on performance optimization, and we're only going to do.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,060
one lecture on it, and we don't have any labs, unfortunately, that really get you to push your limits on this,

6
00:00:37,140 --> 00:00:42,900
which is too bad because it's a very interesting topic, and one I think you'd find yourself well equipped for.

7
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:51,600
There will, there are typically a few small exam problems that are sort of based on some of the material here you'll find in old exams.

8
00:00:52,690 --> 00:01:00,010
But really the idea is, how can I make programs run fast? Given that I've sort of know

9
00:01:00,130 --> 00:01:06,270
what algorithm I'm using and I've perhaps gotten a program that runs, how can

10
00:01:06,270 --> 00:01:15,260
I make it run faster? And one of the themes of it is you can sort of do this in layers. You can sort of

11
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:26,720
first of all do the stuff to avoid sort of things that make programs run slow across a wide

12
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:31,360
variety of machines. And just make your, I would describe it as making your programs run slow across a wide variety of

13
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:36,760
your code more compiler-friendly, and we'll talk about what that means.

14
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:43,860
And you have to have some understanding and appreciation for what compilers are good at and what they're not good at to be able to do that.

15
00:01:43,860 --> 00:01:53,360
And I describe these as the kind of things that you should just be in the habit of when you write programs writing this code that I'll describe as compiler-friendly.

16
00:01:53,360 --> 00:02:01,340
And then the next level is, okay, given that I've sort of taken away the things that really

17
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:04,960
shouldn't have been there in the first place. Now how can I make my programs

18
00:02:04,960 --> 00:02:12,380
run faster? In particular, how can I adapt it to the capabilities of the types of machines

19
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:16,460
that this program is going to run on? And that can again go from ones that will

20
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:22,600
generally make programs run fast across a wide variety of machines to ones that become

21
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:30,460
very specific. And very specific is a risky thing because even in the world of, say, x86 machines,

22
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:35,220
There's quite a variety of them that are available at any given point in time,

23
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:37,080
and they evolve over time as well.

24
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:45,080
So you can make a program run really fast on one particular model of one X-86 processor, but it might

25
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:50,720
not, that if you're trying too hard, you'll find your effort to sort of wasted when you move

26
00:02:50,720 --> 00:02:51,960
it to another.

27
00:02:51,960 --> 00:02:56,760
On the other hand, these general ideas I'm going to describe actually work across quite a range of

28
00:02:56,760 --> 00:02:58,610
machines.

29
00:02:58,610 --> 00:03:00,890
So, and I'll talk about that more as we go along.

30
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:09,750
So it used to be in the bad old days that if you wanted a program to run fast, you had to write it an assembly code.

31
00:03:10,110 --> 00:03:15,350
And that's just plain not true anymore. And if anyone tells you it's true, it's because they're full of it.

32
00:03:15,430 --> 00:03:27,450
It's just not true. Unless, except for the exceptional case where you're running on a very small resource-constrained machine, such as a very small underpowered embedded system.

33
00:03:29,560 --> 00:03:32,180
So let's just assume that we're going to use a compiler.

34
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:36,380
We'll assume for this course we're going to use GCC because it's generally available.

35
00:03:36,640 --> 00:03:39,040
It's not actually the best compiler out there.

36
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:48,060
Intel makes a compiler that costs money to license and stuff, but it's, it really can do some amazing things.

37
00:03:48,060 --> 00:03:53,580
And other compilers exist, but GCC is sort of a good enough compiler for most people.

38
00:03:55,520 --> 00:04:02,680
But there's some features of some things that sort of puzzle compilers that they don't really understand.

39
00:04:02,680 --> 00:04:12,980
Compilers don't really understand, for example, that the numbers you're using, when you say it's an int, might actually range over a much smaller set of values.

40
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:21,060
And they have a very hard time understanding memory referencing patterns and the effect of procedure calls.

41
00:04:26,590 --> 00:04:37,750
And so in general, what happens with the compiler is it has a whole sort of cookbook of optimization strategies and some recipes for,

42
00:04:37,770 --> 00:04:40,710
how to try out different strategies and apply them.

43
00:04:42,090 --> 00:04:49,770
But in general, if it ever feels like this code is something that it doesn't feel confident about being able to make certain transformations,

44
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:51,310
then it just won't.

45
00:04:51,310 --> 00:04:56,710
It will keep things sort of a more direct implementation of exactly what you describe.

46
00:04:56,710 --> 00:04:59,890
And we'll show examples of that as we go along.

47
00:05:00,630 --> 00:05:06,610
So the thing about optimizing compilers, it always has a fallback position, which is to not optimize.

48
00:05:06,610 --> 00:05:11,610
And sometimes that will get in trouble if you want your program to run faster.

49
00:05:11,610 --> 00:05:18,610
And the compiler just, in its own conservative way, decides not to do that optimization.

50
00:05:18,610 --> 00:05:25,610
And one of the tricks that you'll find is pretty useful, now that you can read assembly code, is you run the compiler.

51
00:05:25,610 --> 00:05:28,610
You see what optimizations it does.

52
00:05:28,610 --> 00:05:33,610
And if it doesn't make something that you expect it to be able to do, you go back and figure it out.

53
00:05:33,610 --> 00:05:35,610
So it's very common, by the way, to do.

54
00:05:35,610 --> 00:05:49,600
to rewrite your program in the same language and sort of tune it and up to make it run faster, to make it more compiler-friendly.

55
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:51,200
There's nothing wrong with that.

56
00:05:51,680 --> 00:05:55,840
As long as you don't then just totally obliterate the program and make it totally illegible.

57
00:05:57,490 --> 00:06:01,310
So let's just describe some sort of general optimizations.

58
00:06:01,310 --> 00:06:06,490
And you've actually seen versions of this in some of the assembly code we've already looked

59
00:06:06,570 --> 00:06:13,980
And I'll use mostly sort of examples from multidimensional arrays because

60
00:06:13,980 --> 00:06:17,980
those are actually fairly easy optimization type of tasks.

61
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:21,980
But these apply to other types of program as well.

62
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:29,980
So you saw before when we described how to do array indexing in multidimensional arrays,

63
00:06:29,980 --> 00:06:35,980
that the old style of code was if you had a variable size array,

64
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:47,610
It was up to you, the programmer, to write the formula, of how you convert row I, column J into a position in a one-dimensional array.

65
00:06:47,610 --> 00:06:54,110
So remember, it's just the number of columns times the row number plus the column number is the standard one.

66
00:06:54,110 --> 00:06:58,110
So that would give, this would be pretty typical code then.

67
00:06:58,110 --> 00:07:06,090
It would give you a notation like this, if you wanted to set one row in array a row in array A to the values in a

68
00:07:06,110 --> 00:07:13,110
one-dimensional row B, this is a code you'd write.

69
00:07:13,110 --> 00:07:19,110
And the main observation is within this loop, the only variable that's changing is J.

70
00:07:19,110 --> 00:07:25,530
And so from the array perspective, you're, this computation n times I,

71
00:07:25,530 --> 00:07:32,530
if it gets repeated over and over again within this loop, then you're just wasting, it's a wasted effort.

72
00:07:32,530 --> 00:07:36,510
So you can do what's called code motion, which is to

73
00:07:36,530 --> 00:07:41,930
pre-compute the value of n times i outside of the loop and then use it over and

74
00:07:41,930 --> 00:07:49,440
over again inside and compilers will generally do this when they they can detect, for example,

75
00:07:49,440 --> 00:07:55,200
that it's an array access code and it has this technique. It will generally do

76
00:07:55,200 --> 00:08:03,180
optimizations like this if you set say an optimization level of one or higher to GCCCC.

77
00:08:07,020 --> 00:08:08,520
And we can see this.

78
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:19,540
In fact, this is this code, and I ran it through GCC using optimization 1, and you see as this red instruction shows, it boosted this multiplication outside of the loop.

79
00:08:19,540 --> 00:08:37,500
And it's a little as if you, actually, this code does even more, it turns the code into something that looks more like a pointer code accessing array A and stepping through that element by element of the array.

80
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:54,120
Another one, and we've seen this already, that when GCC turns a multiplication or a division by shifting and adding in operations like that, multiplication or divisions like that, multiplication or division by constants, we've seen examples of that.

81
00:08:54,120 --> 00:09:13,520
And a similar one would happen if we took that program I showed before and applied it to every row, so we wanted it to set for array A, we wanted to set for

82
00:09:13,540 --> 00:09:19,200
of its rows to the value of the one-dimensional array B.

83
00:09:19,200 --> 00:09:26,060
Then again, if we took that code, we boosted the n times i in there.

84
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:31,260
So now the interloop is good, but you realize that this multiplication isn't necessary either,

85
00:09:31,260 --> 00:09:37,740
because what we're doing from i equals 0 to i equals 1 to i equals 2 is we're just increasing

86
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:41,740
the parameter n i by we're adding n to it.

87
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:46,240
So we can, and that's called a reduction in strength.

88
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:56,090
We've taken a multiplication and turned it into addition because there's some predictable pattern of how this variable NI is going to be updated.

89
00:09:56,090 --> 00:10:02,590
Another example, and again, array indexing is a good example for optimizations.

90
00:10:02,590 --> 00:10:11,290
Imagine we had an image that we represent as a two-dimensional array of pixel values.

91
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:13,070
And we want to do something that we want to do something that we represent as a two-dimensional array of pixel values.

92
00:10:13,090 --> 00:10:21,090
that's a filtering operation where we want to take the sum of the four neighbors of a given pixel,

93
00:10:21,090 --> 00:10:27,600
north, south, east, and west, and average those together, or sum them together.

94
00:10:27,600 --> 00:10:36,920
And so the natural way you'd write this and see is to say, I want, I want, usually,

95
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:40,020
images you count from the top down.

96
00:10:40,020 --> 00:10:45,020
And so you'd say this is the pixel above.

97
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:47,020
This is the pixel below.

98
00:10:47,020 --> 00:10:48,020
This is the pixel to the left and this is the pixel to the left,

99
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:50,020
and this is the pixel to the right.

100
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:55,780
And if you do this in and just compile it straight through,

101
00:10:55,780 --> 00:10:57,780
unfortunately,

102
00:10:57,780 --> 00:11:00,780
it appears as if there's three different multiplications

103
00:11:00,780 --> 00:11:04,780
by n, i minus 1, i plus 1, i plus 1, and i plus 1 and i.

104
00:11:04,780 --> 00:11:07,560
And if the compiler isn't too

105
00:11:07,780 --> 00:11:12,780
it won't realize that these are related to each other and it will issue three

106
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:16,850
different multiply operations just to do this one pixel thing.

107
00:11:16,850 --> 00:11:20,850
Whereas if I'm a little more clever, and this is one where I manually

108
00:11:20,850 --> 00:11:24,850
rewrote the code so the compiler would pick it up,

109
00:11:24,850 --> 00:11:30,850
I'd say, well, if IN, so I nj is i times n plus j,

110
00:11:30,850 --> 00:11:37,830
and I can get the pixel above the pixel below by shifting that off

111
00:11:37,850 --> 00:11:42,040
setting that by a value of n.

112
00:11:42,040 --> 00:11:46,540
And then it will issue the code, this will compile with a code with just one multiply.

113
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:51,540
And in general, by the way, multiply used to be a very expensive instruction.

114
00:11:51,540 --> 00:11:57,540
Nowadays, there's enough hardware resources that it takes about three clock cycles, so it's not a huge deal.

115
00:11:57,540 --> 00:12:02,540
But any time you can take three multiplies and use just one instead, that's generally a good idea.

116
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:08,410
Question.

117
00:12:08,410 --> 00:12:11,410
So the question is, what if you're trying to optimize for space?

118
00:12:11,410 --> 00:12:14,390
And there are a lot of optimizations that will

119
00:12:14,410 --> 00:12:19,910
make your code be bigger at the expense of, in order to go faster, right?

120
00:12:19,910 --> 00:12:23,980
This one, though, I'd argue this is actually shorter code, right?

121
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:26,480
And just look at the number of instructions, so.

122
00:12:26,480 --> 00:12:31,980
And usually, so code, you know, that used to be a bigger concern when memory was sort of,

123
00:12:31,980 --> 00:12:37,480
you know, back the original IBM PC had 640 kilobytes of memory, so,

124
00:12:37,480 --> 00:12:42,640
in its maximum configuration, and that was a big deal to actually buy it that much.

125
00:12:42,640 --> 00:12:45,120
So, you know, back then that was a big deal.

126
00:12:45,140 --> 00:12:52,140
But nowadays memory, the size of the program is usually a pretty small fraction of what you're dealing with overall.

127
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:56,860
But it's a valid question.

128
00:12:56,860 --> 00:12:58,860
Okay, so that just shows you an example.

129
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:04,860
And in general, compilers are pretty good at doing those low-level optimizations like that

130
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:07,860
if you write the code in a way that's reasonable.

131
00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:12,860
But there's some other ones that the compiler, and even the fanciest compiler you can buy,

132
00:13:12,860 --> 00:13:15,860
might not be able to figure it out.

133
00:13:15,860 --> 00:13:17,860
And so I like to illustrate this with the compiler.

134
00:13:17,860 --> 00:13:27,020
When the first term we ever taught 213, I was looking at some lab code that some of the students wrote,

135
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:30,020
and I was horrified about this code.

136
00:13:30,020 --> 00:13:33,020
And I showed it to the TAs and none of them figured out what was wrong.

137
00:13:33,020 --> 00:13:39,020
And I've shown it to many other highly trained C programmers, professionals.

138
00:13:39,020 --> 00:13:41,020
And they go, looks okay to me.

139
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:45,700
So let's figure out why I was horrified by this code.

140
00:13:45,700 --> 00:13:47,700
So the idea of this code is supposed to be pretty straightforward.

141
00:13:47,700 --> 00:13:48,700
straightforward.

142
00:13:48,700 --> 00:13:56,700
There's a string S and I want to convert that string, all the characters in it, to lowercase.

143
00:13:56,700 --> 00:14:04,500
So I'm just going to read through the string, and for each string position, test that character.

144
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:10,500
And if it's somewhere between uppercase A and uppercase Z, then I'm going to shift it to

145
00:14:10,500 --> 00:14:14,500
to being between lowercase A and lowercase Z, otherwise I won't change it.

146
00:14:14,500 --> 00:14:16,500
So pretty straightforward.

147
00:14:16,880 --> 00:14:17,880
But if you run this,

148
00:14:17,880 --> 00:14:27,170
this, you see that if you go up to half a million characters, which might sound like a lot,

149
00:14:27,170 --> 00:14:35,190
but it takes 240 or so, so four minutes to run this coat.

150
00:14:35,190 --> 00:14:38,190
And you go, well, that's a pretty big string.

151
00:14:38,190 --> 00:14:39,190
It's really not a big string.

152
00:14:39,190 --> 00:14:45,190
You should be able to do lowercase conversion of a string in a lot less than four seconds.

153
00:14:45,190 --> 00:14:47,190
And you also notice this growth is nonlinear.

154
00:14:47,190 --> 00:14:53,260
linear it's quadratic. It's growing as a square of the string length. So this is not good.

155
00:14:53,260 --> 00:14:58,260
And unfortunately it's the kind, and by the way, this is one of the, that is very easy, surprisingly easy,

156
00:14:58,260 --> 00:15:05,260
to have programs that have some hidden performance bug that makes them run quadratic.

157
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:11,260
And you run tests, and you test for strings of 10,000 or less.

158
00:15:11,260 --> 00:15:17,240
And it doesn't look like a big deal because the runtime is insignificant. So, but then

159
00:15:17,260 --> 00:15:20,260
all of a sudden it hits a really bad case.

160
00:15:20,260 --> 00:15:25,440
So this is a, there's something wrong here.

161
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:30,920
So what's so bad about this program? Well, the key is,

162
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:36,210
uh, went in a test like this of calling sterlin.

163
00:15:36,210 --> 00:15:41,210
So, uh, the way it's determining whether it's reached the end of the string

164
00:15:41,210 --> 00:15:46,520
is by calling sterlin to figure out how long the string is.

165
00:15:46,520 --> 00:15:49,520
And the, uh, now, now, and remember if we do the conversion of a,

166
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:56,340
conversion of a for loop into a go-to form like you've seen, there's various ways to

167
00:15:56,340 --> 00:16:03,500
convert it, but all of them, the test, gets built into the loop. So the main feature of that

168
00:16:03,500 --> 00:16:12,340
is this call to sterling will happen every time you go through the loop. And people overlook

169
00:16:12,340 --> 00:16:19,550
that fact. When you look at the different parts of a for loop, the initialization only gets

170
00:16:19,550 --> 00:16:21,450
executed once, but

171
00:16:21,470 --> 00:16:26,040
both the test and the update get incremented,

172
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:30,040
get applied every time you run through the loop.

173
00:16:30,040 --> 00:16:35,080
So that's getting called as many times as there are characters in the loop,

174
00:16:35,080 --> 00:16:37,590
in the string, right?

175
00:16:37,590 --> 00:16:39,210
And now how does Sterland work?

176
00:16:39,210 --> 00:16:43,050
Remember and see, the only way you know how long a string is,

177
00:16:43,050 --> 00:16:48,090
is to step through the whole thing and find the null character at the end.

178
00:16:48,090 --> 00:16:52,070
So Sterland itself is a linear time operation in the string

179
00:16:52,070 --> 00:16:59,660
And you're doing that, and so you're doing N calls to a function that takes time N.

180
00:17:00,760 --> 00:17:04,100
The string's getting shorter as you go, but not very fast.

181
00:17:04,100 --> 00:17:06,840
So basically that's quadratic performance.

182
00:17:09,080 --> 00:17:12,070
And that explains why you get that runtime.

183
00:17:13,570 --> 00:17:20,330
So in particular, if I just make the following little change, I introduce a local variable called Glenn,

184
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:25,210
and I pre-compute Sterling because the string

185
00:17:25,270 --> 00:17:27,270
the length of the string isn't changing.

186
00:17:27,270 --> 00:17:29,270
I'm just changing the characters in the string.

187
00:17:29,270 --> 00:17:33,780
Then, so the program will do the same thing.

188
00:17:33,780 --> 00:17:37,780
But now the runtime is so short, it doesn't even show up.

189
00:17:37,780 --> 00:17:41,780
It's maybe a second to do a million characters.

190
00:17:41,780 --> 00:17:43,780
It's just not a big deal at all.

191
00:17:43,780 --> 00:17:46,780
As it should be, it's just running through.

192
00:17:46,780 --> 00:17:50,040
And so that's just an example.

193
00:17:50,040 --> 00:17:55,040
One of many that I've seen in my career where something that

194
00:17:55,040 --> 00:18:00,040
that seems almost insignificant turns out to be a serious performance problem.

195
00:18:00,040 --> 00:18:05,860
So why couldn't a compiler figure this out?

196
00:18:05,860 --> 00:18:13,340
Why couldn't a smart compiler look at the

197
00:18:13,340 --> 00:18:22,460
original code and say, you know, this is what the programmer wrote,

198
00:18:22,460 --> 00:18:24,460
but I know a better way to do it.

199
00:18:24,460 --> 00:18:26,460
I'll pre-compute Sterlin in advance.

200
00:18:26,460 --> 00:18:28,520
Well, there's a couple of reasons.

201
00:18:28,520 --> 00:18:32,520
One is, actually, if you look at the code for Sterlin,

202
00:18:32,520 --> 00:18:33,520
you see that you see that, you know,

203
00:18:33,520 --> 00:18:37,520
it's actually modifying the string and Sterland,

204
00:18:37,520 --> 00:18:40,520
I mean, the code here is modifying the string,

205
00:18:40,520 --> 00:18:42,520
and we're calling Sterland on it.

206
00:18:42,520 --> 00:18:45,650
So you'd have to be pretty careful to do the analysis,

207
00:18:45,650 --> 00:18:50,650
the compiler would, to figure out that even though the string is changing,

208
00:18:50,650 --> 00:18:53,810
the result you're going to get from Sterland

209
00:18:53,810 --> 00:18:59,560
is not going to change, right?

210
00:18:59,560 --> 00:19:03,680
So that's one reason.

211
00:19:03,680 --> 00:19:06,870
And the second is, well, and how do,

212
00:19:06,870 --> 00:19:14,870
And how can the compile be sure which version of SterLen is actually going to get used?

